From 7500d5a8a39a3fdd33509676e8a84e27b7427bff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:59:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][CEC] A claim of the logical address that failed is
 asked again, and the losses of the adapter are in kodi.log

Issue #24, a report of 2026-11-26: with the picture and the sound on the
DisplayPort output of an Intel board (a DisplayPort to HDMI converter
with CEC tunnelling), the TV's remote did nothing in Kodi until a video
started, and stopped after another video that changed the display mode
(HLG, HDR). kodi.log had nothing of it.

The CEC adapter of a DisplayPort output (drm_dp_cec.c) needs the HPD of
its display (CEC_CAP_NEEDS_HPD): the kernel takes its logical address
away whenever its physical address goes, at every hot-plug of the
display and every detection of the output by i915, and claims it again
by itself when the address comes back, with polls on the line. A claim
that gets no answer (the port's IRQ_HPD masked after an HPD storm, which
that PC's kernel log shows on DP-1; a busy line) fails, with
CEC_LOG_ADDRS_FL_CONFIG_FAILED, and nothing asks again until the address
changes once more: libCEC does not look at the logical addresses in the
state changes of the adapter (its "TODO: handle
ev.state_change.log_addr_mask change"). The adapter then acknowledges
nothing the TV sends it, and the kernel refuses what libCEC sends.

The thread of the adapter now looks at a kernel adapter once a second
while libCEC uses it (CPeripheralCecAdapter::ProcessAdapterState(),
PeripheralCecState.h):

- a claim that failed is asked again with the logical addresses libCEC
  set (CEC_ADAP_S_LOG_ADDRS, on a file that does not block), after 2, 4,
  8, 16 and then every 30 s, until it works;
- a loss of the logical address of 2 s or more is said in kodi.log, with
  why (no physical address: the display is not there; a claim under way;
  a claim that failed; the adapter gone), and its end, with how long it
  lasted and how many shorter losses came before it, which are only
  counted (one comes with every detection of the output);
- a logical address other than the one before the loss, which libCEC
  still sends from, opens the connection again.

Kodi is not made the active source again by itself after a loss: while
the adapter has no logical address the kernel drops what it receives,
so a change of the input on the TV or a receiver meanwhile is not known,
and that would take the input back from the user. The kernel says the
physical address of the PC again once the claim works (<Report Physical
Address>), as at libCEC's open.

TestPeripheralCecState: the phases of an adapter, a short loss counted,
a failed claim asked again with its waits, another logical address, an
adapter that libCEC does not use, a long loss without a failed claim.
